# Dependencies

## Context

`app/package.json` currently declares exactly two devDependencies:
`typescript` and `vitest`, and zero runtime dependencies. The store, reducer,
and text lib are all hand-rolled on purpose (see `architecture.mdc`,
`custom-lib.mdc`) — pulling in a package is often an attempt to shortcut
around that constraint.

## Rule

- Do NOT add, remove, or upgrade any package in `app/package.json` (or run
  `npm install <pkg>`) unless the user has explicitly approved that specific
  package in the current request.
- Do NOT introduce a dependency to replace in-house code (e.g. a state
  library instead of `store.ts`, lodash instead of `app/src/lib/text.ts`,
  a test framework instead of vitest).
- If a task seems to need a new package, stop and ask the user first, naming
  the package and why.

## How to verify

1. `git diff -- app/package.json app/package-lock.json` — empty, unless the
   user explicitly approved a specific package in this session.
2. `cat app/package.json` — `dependencies` is absent/empty and
   `devDependencies` contains only `typescript` and `vitest`, unless approved
   otherwise.
3. `cd app && npm test && npm run typecheck` still pass without any new
   `node_modules` entries being required.
